# Missing hashed build assets must 404 for real — never fall back to the SPA
# shell here. Without this rule, Cloudflare Pages' automatic SPA fallback
# (no top-level 404.html in this deploy) serves index.html with a 200 for
# ANY unmatched path, including a since-removed/renamed chunk under
# /assets/*. That wrong response still inherits the `/assets/*` header rule
# in public/_headers (Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable),
# so Cloudflare's edge caches the mistaken 200-HTML response as if it were
# the real JS file — for up to a year. The next browser to request that
# exact chunk URL gets HTML back for a <script type="module"> load and fails
# with "Failed to load module script: ... MIME type of 'text/html'".
#
# The destination MUST be a distinct path from the source. The previous
# version of this rule (/assets/* -> /assets/:splat 404) was a same-shape
# rewrite that Cloudflare silently no-ops, falling through to the /* catch-
# all below — confirmed live: requests for a never-before-seen fake asset
# path still came back 200 text/html after that version shipped. Routing to
# a genuinely different file is what makes the 404 real.
/assets/*  /_asset-404.html  404

# Client-side routes (React Router) — always serve the SPA shell.
/*  /index.html  200
